Goal: Build a contact page where a visitor enters their first name, last name, email address, and message. The application saves the submission and displays a confirmation page.

| ID    | Requirement                                                          |
| ----- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| FR-01 | A `GET` request to the application URL displays the contact form.    |
| FR-02 | The form collects first name, last name, email address, and message. |
| FR-03 | Submitting the form sends a `POST` request containing those fields.  |
| FR-04 | The application validates the submission and saves it to DynamoDB.   |
| FR-05 | A successful save displays a confirmation page.                      |
| FR-06 | A failed save displays an error and does not claim success.          |
Architecture

The visitor accesses a regional API Gateway REST API. Its GET and POST methods use Lambda proxy integration with a Python Lambda function. Lambda serves the HTML pages and writes submissions to a DynamoDB table. The deployed API stage provides the application URL.

Acceptance criteria
Opening the URL displays the contact form.
Submitting valid details creates an item in DynamoDB with the entered first name, last name, email, and message.
The confirmation page appears only after the write succeeds.
Missing or invalid fields produce a clear error.
A database failure produces an error response and can be investigated through Lambda logs.

Design decision before implementation: The video uses email as the DynamoDB partition key and says duplicate emails are rejected. A partition key alone does not reject duplicates: a normal write can replace an existing item. If each email may submit only once, use a conditional write. If repeat messages are allowed, use a unique submission ID as the key.

For deployment, give Lambda logging permission and write access limited to this table. The transcript demonstrates broad DynamoDB permissions, but the application only requires access to its own table.
